Let $q = n/m$, so $n = mq$.

**Claim:** The minimal polynomial of $\alpha^m$ over $K$ is $g(x) = x^{q} - a$.

**Proof.**

Since $\alpha$ is a root of $f(x)=x^n-a$, we have $\alpha^n = a$. Therefore
$$(\alpha^m)^q = \alpha^{mq} = \alpha^n = a,$$
so $\alpha^m$ is a root of $g(x)=x^q - a \in K[x]$. Hence the minimal polynomial of $\alpha^m$ over $K$ divides $g$, giving
$$[K(\alpha^m):K] \leq \deg g = q. \tag{1}$$

Since $f(x)=x^n-a$ is irreducible over $K$, it is the minimal polynomial of $\alpha$, so
$$[K(\alpha):K] = n. \tag{2}$$

Now $\alpha$ satisfies $x^m - \alpha^m \in K(\alpha^m)[x]$, so
$$[K(\alpha):K(\alpha^m)] \leq m. \tag{3}$$

By the tower law,
$$[K(\alpha):K] = [K(\alpha):K(\alpha^m)]\cdot [K(\alpha^m):K].$$

Using (2) and (3),
$$n \leq m \cdot [K(\alpha^m):K],$$
so
$$[K(\alpha^m):K] \geq \frac{n}{m} = q. \tag{4}$$

Combining (1) and (4),
$$[K(\alpha^m):K] = q.$$

Since $g(x)=x^q-a$ is a monic polynomial of degree $q$ having $\alpha^m$ as a root, and the degree of the minimal polynomial equals $[K(\alpha^m):K]=q=\deg g$, it follows that $g$ is itself the minimal polynomial of $\alpha^m$ over $K$.

$$\boxed{\text{The minimal polynomial of } \alpha^m \text{ over } K \text{ is } x^{\,n/m}-a.}$$

